    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media across the spectrum agree that the Ukraine war has made a rapid transformation of the Bundeswehr unavoidable, with repeated calls for higher defence budgets, accelerated procurement and a stronger focus on cyber and civil-protection capabilities to meet NATO's 2 percent target. At the same time, there is a broadly shared demand for tighter export-control rules, especially concerning arms sales to the Middle East and to actors linked to the Iran-Russia conflict, reflecting a common concern that German weapons should not fuel further instability. Finally, most outlets, regardless of political leaning, stress the need for structural reforms such as reinstating a form of conscription or a citizen-reserve to address personnel shortages and improve overall readiness.",
      "controversial_points": "German media split between security-first conservatives demanding higher NATO spending and left-liberals prioritizing diplomatic conflict resolution. Conscription debate pits nationalist calls for citizen army against liberal concerns over militarization of society. Arms-export coverage reveals tension between economic-liberal industry interests and ethical opposition to sales to authoritarian regimes. Cyber-defence and civil protection discussions expose clash between federal centralisation advocates and states demanding more autonomy."
